body:
These are the old NTSC/PAL luminance weights. They should probably be the Rec. 709 luminance weights. I recommend labelling them as such so that they will be easy to adapt to other colour primaries in the future (https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12783):

```suggestion
		// Tone map the SSR color to have smoother roughness filtering across samples with varying luminance.
		const vec3 rec709_luminance_weights = vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722);
		color.rgb /= 1.0 + dot(color.rgb, rec709_luminance_weights);
```

I also notice that there's an asymptote at -1.0. I don't see any clipping of colour to be non-negative, so is it possible that negative lights could create a high negative `color` value that would behave badly with this?

Otherwise this looks fine: I don't see any issue with "tone mapping" colour to `1.0` relative luminance during this processing, given that it is inverted later, even with HDR output.

